<!-- Ubigi vs Airhub Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set ubigiPrimary = providerA.codes | findCode(providerA.primaryCode) %}
  {% set airhubPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}
  {% set airhubBest = providerB.codes | findCode("ESIMOFFER5") %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Ubigi</strong> if you want an established telecom behind your eSIM and might actually want a recurring monthly plan for a country you visit often. Pick <strong>Airhub</strong> if you just want the lowest per-GB price and don't mind a bare-bones data app. On a straight price comparison Airhub wins almost everywhere; Ubigi's case rests entirely on who runs it and what else it sells, not on being cheap.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Telecom Pedigree vs Rock-Bottom Pricing</h2>
  <p>
    Ubigi is a brand of Transatel, a French MVNO enabler running cellular connectivity since the early 2000s and now part of NTT Group. The same underlying network also powers the built-in SIM connectivity in connected cars from BMW, Toyota, Jeep, Maserati, Fiat, Alfa Romeo, Mini, Jaguar, and Land Rover. Airhub has no equivalent backstory: it's a straightforward budget eSIM app that sells data in multi-gigabyte blocks (3GB, 4GB) instead of single-GB units, and undercuts almost everyone on this site in cheaper destinations.
  </p>
  <p>
    That pedigree doesn't make Ubigi cheaper. In Japan, where the plan sizes actually line up at 1GB each, Ubigi charges $4 against Airhub's $1.55, roughly 2.6 times more for the identical amount of data. Airhub also bundles SMS and, in many destinations, a local phone number into its plans at no extra cost, something Ubigi doesn't offer at all. Neither provider includes voice calls.
  </p>

